Date: Sun, 2 Aug 2026 07:30:57 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 6/7] camera: choose the viewfinder resolution, and remember it

A camera offers sizes it cannot always deliver. Asked for one of those, this
pipeline either refuses outright - 'Failed to start streaming: Resource busy'
- or, worse, starts without complaint and delivers nothing at all, leaving a
black rectangle where the viewfinder should be. Measured on a Fairphone 3,
each size in its own process from idle: of the 47 sizes that camera
advertises, everything below about 1.8 megapixels fails, while 1600x1200,
1920x1080 and 3840x2400 work.

Which ones work therefore cannot be derived, only tried. On first use the
viewfinder bisects the list - which is ordered by pixel count - for the
smallest size that works: bisecting matters, because walking up from the
smallest would have cost thirty pipeline restarts on the camera above, in
front of a user looking at a black screen, where six suffice.

What counts as working is buffers arriving at the video sink, not the absence
of an error. That distinction was learned the hard way: judged by errors
alone, the search settled on 1024x600 - which reports nothing, streams
nothing, and shows a black viewfinder for ever. A size that produces no
pictures within the settling time is treated exactly like one that refused.

A size that was chosen rather than found is checked the same way, since it may
come from an older version, a different camera, or a person picking from the
menu; if nothing arrives, the stored choice is dropped and the search runs.

The winner is published as the preview-resolution property, and the
application binds that to a setting in both directions, so the search happens
once in the lifetime of an installation rather than at every start.

Smallest-first is deliberate. It is the cheapest to debayer in software and so
the fastest preview, and the picture is taken at the largest size the source
offers regardless of what the viewfinder runs at.
